STEP 6 — Patient Validation

Validation functions are added to:

hospital/js/validation.js

Reason:

validation.js already owns validation responsibilities
for login and signup.

Patient-specific validation functions are kept separate
from login validation functions.

Functions introduced:

isValidName()
    Validates basic alphabetic names.

isValidMobile()
    Requires exactly 10 digits.

isValidPin()
    Requires exactly 6 digits.

isValidDateOfBirth()
    Requires a date that is not in the future.

isValidMeasurement()
    Allows an empty value because height and weight are
    optional, but if provided the value must be greater
    than zero.


Patient form behavior:

hospital/js/patient-form.js

The form:

1. Reads values from HTML inputs.
2. Trims text values where appropriate.
3. Runs validation.
4. Stops immediately when validation fails.
5. Displays a friendly validation message.

event.preventDefault()

prevents the browser from performing its normal form
submission, which would reload/navigate the page.

The form currently does NOT save the patient.

Current flow:

Form
    ↓
Read values
    ↓
Validate
    ↓
Display result

Next flow:

Form
    ↓
Read values
    ↓
Validate
    ↓
Create patient object
    ↓
Save patient


Important concept:

Validation functions return true/false.

Example:

isValidMobile("9876543210")
    → true

isValidMobile("12345")
    → false

The form decides what message to show.

This keeps validation rules separate from UI messages.


Current limitation:

Name validation currently accepts only English
alphabetic characters and spaces.

This is intentionally simple for the prototype.

Do not add complicated international name handling
unless the requirement requires it.


Testing:

Test each validation rule independently.

Do not move to patient storage until the validation
behavior is working correctly.


STEP 7 — Creating a Patient Object

The New Patient form now converts the entered form values
into a JavaScript patient object.

File:

hospital/js/patient-form.js

The form first reads the values from the HTML controls.

Example:

document.getElementById("firstName").value

The form then validates the values.

Only after validation succeeds is the patient object created.


PATIENT OBJECT

A patient object contains:

patientId
firstName
lastName
dob
gender
bloodGroup
height
weight
mobile
email
address
city
state
pin
emergencyContact
createdAt
createdBy
deleted


The emergency contact is grouped into its own object:

emergencyContact:
    name
    relationship
    mobile


AUDIT INFORMATION

createdAt:
    Time at which the patient was created.

createdBy:
    Logged-in user who created the patient.

getCurrentUser() is used for createdBy.


SOFT DELETE PREPARATION

New patients contain:

deleted: false

This will later allow US004 to use soft deletion.

A deleted patient can become:

deleted: true

without removing the object from storage.

This helps preserve patient history.


PATIENT CREATION FLOW

Form submission
    ↓
preventDefault()
    ↓
Read form values
    ↓
Validate
    ↓
Create patient object
    ↓
addPatient(patient)
    ↓
localStorage


addPatient()

belongs to:

patient-data.js

It is responsible for adding the patient to the stored
patient array.

patient-form.js does not directly manipulate localStorage.

This is an important SRP boundary.


JSON / localStorage

localStorage cannot directly store JavaScript objects.

patient-data.js handles:

JSON.stringify()
    JavaScript object/array → JSON string

and:

JSON.parse()
    JSON string → JavaScript object/array


Testing:

After creating a patient, run:

getPatients()

in the browser console.

The result should be an array containing the new patient.

You can also inspect:

